= Computability — Technical =

== Partial computable functions ==
A Turing machine can define a partial function:

<pre>
φ : Σ* ⇀ Σ*
</pre>

where nontermination corresponds to undefined output.

A total computable function halts on every input in its domain.

== Decidable and recognizable sets ==
A language L is decidable if some machine halts on every input and decides membership.

L is recognizable if some machine accepts members, while nonmembers may be rejected or loop forever.

== Reductions ==
Computability reductions compare problem difficulty.

If A reduces effectively to B, a solver for B can be used to solve A.

Reductions are central to undecidability proofs.

== Church–Turing thesis ==
The thesis concerns effective calculability, while equivalence proofs among Turing machines, lambda calculus and recursive functions are formal theorems.

These claims should not be conflated.

